---
import { Badge } from '@/components/ui/badge';
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/hakula139/assistant-bench';
const docs = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/design`;

const sections = {
  rounds: 'How a round works',
  scores: 'Scores and intervals',
  leaderboard: 'Reading the leaderboard',
  leader: 'How the leader is chosen',
  diagnostics: 'Round diagnostics',
  limits: 'Known limits',
} as const;

const columns = [
  {
    term: 'Rank',
    definition:
      'The range of ranks the intervals allow, from counting only the systems clearly above to counting every system that could be.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Preliminary',
    badge: true,
    definition:
      'Fewer than 40 decisive votes, meaning votes won by either side. The plot draws it as a hollow dot, and its score may still move.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Unranked',
    definition:
      'Listed below the table without a score, because too few comparisons connect the system to the reference to bound its score.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Votes',
    definition:
      "The rater's votes on the system. Results settled without a vote count toward the score but not here.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Both bad',
    definition:
      'The share of those votes that judged both outputs bad, which shows the absolute shortfall a relative score hides.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Gate failures',
    definition: "The share of the system's outputs that failed a gate.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Cost / output',
    definition: 'The mean cost of one output at list prices.',
  },
  {
    term: 'p50 latency',
    definition: 'The median time from the request to the complete response.',
  },
];

const diagnostics = [
  {
    term: 'Rater agreement',
    definition:
      'How often a side-swapped repeat matched the original vote. Below 70%, the intervals overstate how well systems separate.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Separated pairs',
    definition: 'The share of system pairs whose intervals do not overlap.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Split-half τ',
    definition:
      'How closely the orders fit to odd and to even sessions agree, where 1 means the same order.',
  },
];

const h2 = 'scroll-mt-6 text-lg font-semibold';
const dl = 'grid gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-6 sm:gap-y-2';
const list = 'space-y-2 pl-6 marker:text-muted-foreground';
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_13rem] lg:justify-between lg:gap-x-16 lg:gap-y-10">
    <header class="space-y-3 lg:col-start-1">
      <h1 class="text-[1.75rem] leading-tight font-semibold">Methodology</h1>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Every system in a family answers the same cases, and one rater compares the outputs two at a
        time without seeing which system wrote which. The design note on{' '}
        <a href={`${docs}/ranking.md`}>ranking</a> has the math behind this page.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav
      aria-label="On this page"
      class="text-sm lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:col-start-2 lg:row-span-2 lg:row-start-1 lg:self-start"
    >
      <p class="mb-2 font-medium">On this page</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1.5">
        {Object.entries(sections).map(([id, title]) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${id}`} class="text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground">
              {title}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="space-y-10 leading-relaxed lg:col-start-1">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="rounds" class={h2}>
          {sections.rounds}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            Every system answers the family's cases with the same instruction. Changing a system's
            model, effort, harness, or prompt makes it a new system.
          </li>
          <li>
            Gates check each output for problems such as truncation or the wrong script. A failing
            output loses without a vote, and two identical outputs tie.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rater sees two outputs side by side without system names and picks the better one, a
            tie, or both bad. Sides are counterbalanced, and some pairs return later with the sides
            swapped to check the rater's consistency.
          </li>
          <li>
            Closing a round refits every vote so far, so each round shows the cumulative board on
            that date.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="scores" class={h2}>
          {sections.scores}
        </h2>
        <p>
          A Bradley-Terry model turns the votes into scores on an Elo-like scale, with the family's
          reference system at 1,000. Ties and both-bad votes count as half a win, and a separate
          term absorbs any bias toward the left pane.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 95% intervals come from 1,000 bootstrap resamples of whole cases, since votes on one
          case are correlated. Each category's board refits that category's votes alone, so its
          intervals are wider and it shows no rank.
        </p>
        <p>
          A vote stays on record but leaves the fit when its case is retired, or when either output
          was removed, now fails a gate, or now settles the pair without a vote. The round summary
          counts these votes as left out of the fit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Families scored by tests rank systems by the share of trials that pass, counting errored
          trials as failures.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leaderboard" class={h2}>
          {sections.leaderboard}
        </h2>
        <p>The plot draws each 95% interval on one shared axis, with a rule at the reference.</p>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {columns.map(({ term, badge, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">
                {badge ? (
                  <Badge variant="outline" className="font-normal text-muted-foreground">
                    {term}
                  </Badge>
                ) : (
                  term
                )}
              </dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          A dash marks a missing value. In a category view, the last four columns stay family-wide.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leader" class={h2}>
          {sections.leader}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            A ranked system ties with the top one when the 95% interval of their score difference
            contains 0 or is unbounded. With no tie, the top system leads.
          </li>
          <li>
            While any tied system is preliminary, the round names no leader, so a system with few
            votes can neither take the lead nor lose a tie-break.
          </li>
          <li>
            Otherwise the tie goes to the lower cost per output, then the lower median latency, then
            the higher score, with a known value beating a missing one.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="diagnostics" class={h2}>
          {sections.diagnostics}
        </h2>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {diagnostics.map(({ term, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">{term}</dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="limits" class={h2}>
          {sections.limits}
        </h2>
        <ul class:list={['list-disc', list]}>
          <li>One person casts every vote, so each board reflects that person's preferences.</li>
          <li>Scores are relative, and the both-bad rate is the only absolute signal.</li>
          <li>
            Votes are few, so only clear preferences separate, and neighbors often share a rank
            range.
          </li>
          <li>
            Scores carry no length adjustment, though each system page shows how much length moves a
            score.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          The design notes on <a href={`${docs}/ranking.md`}>ranking</a>, the{' '}
          <a href={`${docs}/arena.md`}>vote arena</a>, and each{' '}
          <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/docs/design/families`}>family</a> cover the details.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
